Oxide Dialect

Ingredients: AMBIENT TECHNO & IDM + JAZZ RAP / NATIVE TONGUE + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + YAMAHA DX7
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a water-damaged, melted cassette tape excavated from a 1993 municipal time capsule.

Oxide Dialect sounds like an open-window summer cipher drifting across an empty planetarium. The knotty, non-quantized cadences and Afrocentric playfulness of classic Native Tongue jazz rap are disassembled and floated inside the cavernous, weightless spaces of ambient techno. Crisp FM chime clusters and glassy DX7 frequency-modulation reeds wobble against micro-glitched breakbeats, while murmured, polyrhythmic verses trace constellations through the hiss of decaying tape.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 78–92 BPM (felt in half-time suspension). Beats eschew the standard four-on-the-floor kick; instead, sub-bass thuds drop unpredictably between swung, un-quantized hi-hat micro-fractures, giving the illusion of a slow heartbeat floating inside zero gravity.
*   Harmonic Palette: Lydian and Dorian modes mapped through non-tempered microtonal tunings. Lush extended jazz chords (maj9#11, m11) are deconstructed so that only the highest harmonic overtones shimmer against resonant sub-frequencies.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Subverted DX7 Glass:* The Yamaha DX7 is stripped of its clean 1980s pop sheen; operators are programmed into unstable feedback algorithms to simulate warped upright bass strings and rusted vibraphones.
       Oxide-Cavitation Gating (Impossible Technique):* Vocals and master buses are re-amped through actual decaying ferric tape ribbons soaked in warm saltwater; the physical tears and flaking iron oxide act as unpredictable dynamic gates, muting frequencies at random intervals to generate rhythmic micro-silences.
       Spatial Negative-Space Reverb:* Rather than flooding the mix with long decay tails, reverbs are reverse-sidechained to the spoken vocals—vast acoustic halos collapse instantly into utter dry silence whenever a syllable hits, creating an airy, hyper-intimate suction effect.
*   Dynamics: Tracks begin in microscopic, crackling quietude, gradually accumulating layers of staggered spoken cadences and DX7 Rhodes-like vapor before evaporating abruptly, leaving solitary bass hums and cassette deck mechanical noise.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the late autumn of 2024, an unauthorized dig into a 1993 concrete vault buried beneath an abandoned community center in South London yielded a corroded Maxell XLII tape labeled "Cipher Orbit '93". When a group of sound designers and MCs salvaged the melted spool, the audio was half-erased: complex jazz-rap rhymes had fused permanently into degraded synth sweeps and sub-audible techno hums. Inspired by the artifact's ghostly beauty, a subculture formed on the gravel rooftops of council estates. Dressing in oversized 1990s color-blocked windbreakers patched with thermal mylar space blankets and wearing broken vintage watch movements as jewelry, these "Oxide Poets" drag telescopes, battery-powered DX7s, and battery amps onto rooftops at dusk. Their cyphers are quiet and introspective; participants pass a handheld field recorder in total darkness, speaking to the night sky rather than to each other while celestial tracking apps hum on muted smartphones.

Typical Instruments: Yamaha DX7 (corrupted Sysex algorithm banks, custom microtonal scales), Roland TR-606 (run through analog bit-crushers), upright bass played exclusively with cracked wooden bows, handheld condenser mics, modified four-track cassette recorders, and portable telescope tripods struck as percussion.

For Fans Of: A Tribe Called Quest, Biosphere, Digable Planets, Autechre

